progressively consumed the enormous energy reserves they originally contained in the

3
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:26,900
form of uranium. Not natural uranium, but as in nearly all nuclear power stations in the world,

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:39,140
enriched uranium, the fruit of long preparation. It all begins in the mine, underground, or opencast.

5
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:50,419
A relatively poor ore, one to three kilograms of uranium per tonne, which has to be concentrated

6
00:00:50,519 --> 00:00:58,939
and purified. It is sorted, ground and submitted to a whole sequence of

7
00:00:59,039 --> 00:01:05,260
chemical treatments in which the uranium changes form and color many times.

8
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:15,139
It appears as yellow cake or uranium concentrate, then ammonium urinate, now as

9
00:01:15,239 --> 00:01:34,239
trioxide, here as tetrafluoride, and finally as uranium hexafluoride, a gas which is crystallised

10
00:01:34,339 --> 00:01:40,499
for shipment in these containers. It is ready to be submitted to a much deeper transformation

11
00:01:40,599 --> 00:01:42,999
of quite another nature: enrichment.

12
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:20,620
Behind this cooling tower, a huge plant. It can supply a hundred or so nuclear power stations

13
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:29,440
of the 1,000 megawatt range. More than a quarter of world enrichment capacity. These buildings

14
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:34,760
are so wide and so long that they appear squat.

15
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:37,020
Yet they are around 40 meters high.

16
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,840
In these containers, uranium hexafluoride,

17
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:56,120
natural uranium, soon to be introduced into this plant for a kind of sorting process

18
00:02:56,220 --> 00:02:59,300
on an infinitely small scale, the scale of the atom.

19
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:09,660
Natural uranium contains two isotopes, uranium-235

20
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:13,620
and uranium-238.

21
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:24,040
Uranium-235, which will undergo fission, is far the less abundant, only 0.7%.

22
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:29,040
Thus, in one tonne of uranium in the natural state, there are only seven kilograms of uranium-235.

23
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:39,040
Light-water reactors require uranium four to five times richer, with up to 3.2 percent of uranium-235.

24
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:42,040
Thirty-two kilograms per tonne.

25
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:46,040
That is twenty-five more than in natural uranium.

26
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:51,040
Such enrichment can only be obtained at the expense of further tonnes of uranium.

27
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:58,880
uranium. To enrich this tonne of uranium by adding 25 kilograms of uranium-235,

28
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:07,040
other tonnes must be depleted and as much as possible. If we were content to

29
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:12,579
extract one kilogram of uranium-235 from every tonne, we'd need 25 tonnes of

30
00:04:12,679 --> 00:04:18,600
natural uranium. That's a lot because uranium is expensive. In practice, about

31
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:23,100
About 5 kilograms of uranium-235 can be extracted from every tonne,

32
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:28,900
thus it's enough to deplete only 5 tonnes of uranium in an exchange to modify the 2,3,5 content,

33
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,499
initially 7 kilograms per tonne.

34
00:04:35,399 --> 00:04:40,900
On the one hand, we obtain 1 tonne containing 32 kilograms of uranium-235,

35
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,600
uranium at 3.2 percent.

36
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:48,100
And on the other hand, 5 tonnes containing not more than 2 kilograms

37
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:53,540
uranium at zero two percent. The enriched uranium on the one side

38
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:57,220
and the depleted uranium on the other.

39
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:02,179
The goal of the enrichment plant is to effect such an exchange with natural

40
00:05:02,279 --> 00:05:07,059
uranium as point of departure. This exchange takes

41
00:05:07,159 --> 00:05:10,820
place progressively due to a series of modules which enrich

42
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:14,179
and deplete at the same time.

43
00:05:14,279 --> 00:05:18,179
The uranium fed in is separated into two equal parts.

44
00:05:18,279 --> 00:05:24,679
It comes out on one side slightly more enriched, with 2 per 1,000 of uranium 235 more.

45
00:05:24,779 --> 00:05:31,179
And on the other side, slightly less enriched, with 2 per 1,000 of uranium 235 less.

46
00:05:31,279 --> 00:05:36,179
These slight variations mean that the operation must be repeated hundreds of times

47
00:05:36,279 --> 00:05:39,179
in similar modules working in series.

48
00:05:39,279 --> 00:05:45,179
The enriched uranium from each of these is conveyed to the inlet of the following module.

49
00:05:45,279 --> 00:05:51,179
While the uranium that comes out depleted is fed back to the preceding module.

50
00:05:55,279 --> 00:06:00,179
Thus each module is fed both by the uranium enriched in the preceding module

51
00:06:00,279 --> 00:06:05,179
and by the uranium depleted in the next module up the line.

52
00:06:05,279 --> 00:06:10,699
Both flows have the same uranium-235 content.

53
00:06:10,799 --> 00:06:14,840
These modules, thus linked, make up what is known as a cascade.

54
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:21,780
Progressive continuous exchange gives us an increase in uranium-235 content in one direction,

55
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:26,759
compensated for by a reverse movement of uranium-238.

56
00:06:26,859 --> 00:06:33,340
We enrich in one direction and deplete in the other.

57
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:40,179
Within the plant there are 1,400 modules, 1,400 stages and while each stage enriches

58
00:06:40,279 --> 00:06:46,940
and depletes at the same time we may say that 800 are enriching uranium and 600 depleting

59
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,720
it.

60
00:06:49,820 --> 00:06:57,540
For natural uranium is supplied at about the middle of the cascade.

61
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:04,739
from this uranium at 0.71%. On the one hand we have the 800 stages needed to obtain the

62
00:07:04,839 --> 00:07:14,040
desired degree of enrichment 3.25%. But this uranium can only be enriched through the uranium

63
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:19,540
235 extracted from the other side of the cascade and if we need 600 stages on the depletion

64
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:25,040
side it is to get the most out of the uranium and finally reject uranium depleted down to

65
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:32,660
point two percent. Don't forget that the more we deplete the less natural uranium

66
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:37,999
we need at the entry to the process. If we only need to feed in six tons of

67
00:07:38,099 --> 00:07:43,499
natural uranium to produce one ton of enriched uranium it is because we reject

68
00:07:43,599 --> 00:07:51,080
the uranium depleted two point two percent five tons of it. This is the

69
00:07:51,180 --> 00:08:00,580
principle of the cascade. But what exactly are these stages? There are large elements

70
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:10,080
about 20 metres high linked by condites, some going in the enrichment direction and others

71
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:20,320
in the depletion direction. Isotope separation takes place in the upper part, the diffuser.

72
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:24,460
In these tubes, where the uranium circulates in the gaseous state.

73
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:29,540
Thus the process is known as gaseous diffusion.

74
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:34,420
This is why uranium hexafluoride, UF6, is used.

75
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:39,859
It remains in the gaseous state at low temperatures, above 55 degrees centigrade.

76
00:08:39,959 --> 00:08:46,900
In each gas molecule, the uranium atom is surrounded by six fluorine atoms.

77
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,840
All of mass 19.

78
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:59,399
But there are two possible masses for the uranium atom, 238 or 235.

79
00:08:59,499 --> 00:09:07,980
Total molecular mass is thus 352 or 349, a difference of three and about 350.

80
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:10,840
This is very little to differentiate between the two isotopes.

81
00:09:10,940 --> 00:09:15,040
Now let's see how we go about it.

82
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:21,560
In a gas, the constantly colliding molecules have the same kinetic energy.

83
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:27,240
This means that the heavier molecules, containing uranium-238, move slower than the lighter

84
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:34,760
molecules, containing uranium-235.

85
00:09:34,860 --> 00:09:41,500
The faster molecules strike the wall of the containing vessel more frequently.

86
00:09:41,599 --> 00:09:46,100
If micropores are put in the wall, the lighter molecules will have more chance

87
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,980
to go through than the heavier ones.

88
00:09:57,379 --> 00:10:03,399
This leads to slight enrichment of the gas after passage through the wall.

89
00:10:03,499 --> 00:10:06,619
These porous walls, known as diffusion barriers,

90
00:10:06,719 --> 00:10:16,259
take the form of long tubes with billions holes per square centimetre. The secret of this technique lies in the manufacture of

91
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:23,720
these porous barriers. In each diffuser there are several thousand such barriers between

92
00:10:26,839 --> 00:10:33,739
two circular plaques seven metres apart. The gas inlet is at the same level as the outlet.

93
00:10:33,839 --> 00:10:40,739
The gas rises in the up-going tubes, reaching an upper ring shaped area where it is channelled

94
00:10:42,619 --> 00:10:49,519
to the down-going tubes. There are fewer down-going than up-going tubes, since gas flow progressively

95
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:55,639
diminishes over the journey. Flow is half as much at the outlet. This is the depleted

96
00:10:55,739 --> 00:11:02,639
fraction of the gas. The other half is diffused through the barriers becoming enriched. The

97
00:11:02,739 --> 00:11:12,759
the enriched gas appears in the low pressure central it is sucked down by a compressor and

98
00:11:12,859 --> 00:11:17,519
sent to the next diffuser at slightly under atmospheric pressure this has the effect of

99
00:11:17,619 --> 00:11:25,960
heating it a few dozen degrees each stage is composed of three distinct parts the upper

100
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:32,440
part, the diffuser, with the hexafluoride inlet conduit, and the depleted gas evacuation

101
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:41,780
conduit. The enriched gas goes down inside the stage. At the bottom is the compressor

102
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:49,700
to send the gas to the next stage. Between these two units, a heat exchanger compensates

103
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:54,940
for gas heating at each compression.

104
00:11:55,040 --> 00:12:00,680
The gas is cooled by contact with the pipe network of a special water cooling circuit.

105
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:12,600
But let's return to gas circulation in the cascade stages.

106
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:17,940
Each diffuser is associated with the compressor at the base of the preceding diffuser because

107
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:23,320
it is the one which supplies it with the uranium hexafluoride. The stage is completed with

108
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:33,980
the heat exchanger. Such is the complete stage. The diffuser is fed both by the enriched gas

109
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:43,800
from the preceding diffuser and by the depleted gas from the following diffuser. A conduit

110
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:50,120
It feeds it back to the same compressor.

111
00:12:50,220 --> 00:12:54,520
This is how the cascade's various enrichment stages are hooked up.

112
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:58,420
The size of the elements is impressive.

113
00:12:58,520 --> 00:13:02,880
This is just the upper part of a stage, the diffuser.

114
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:28,280
This is the base, housing the compressor, and the intermediate part, the heat exchanger,

115
00:13:28,380 --> 00:13:36,940
containing the cooling tubes.

116
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:43,580
In the plant, the stages are set out in vast adjacent halls, each with a group of 20 stages.

117
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:51,580
The uranium hexafluoride from the preceding group is moved by an initial compressor, the

118
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:53,879
only one not topped by a diffuser.

119
00:13:53,979 --> 00:13:59,139
The gas becomes more and more enriched through the two ranks of stages until reaching the

120
00:13:59,239 --> 00:14:03,440
last diffuser which has no compressor.

121
00:14:03,540 --> 00:14:08,700
The gas which comes out is fed to the compressor on the next group.

122
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:12,239
Feedback of depleted gas is in the opposite direction.

123
00:14:12,339 --> 00:14:17,960
time conduit link is between the two ranks of stages and not on the outside.

124
00:14:18,060 --> 00:14:22,879
Let's take a closer look at gas circulation.

125
00:14:24,979 --> 00:14:33,159
There are two flows in opposite directions, one towards enrichment, the

126
00:14:33,259 --> 00:14:42,220
other towards depletion, progressing from group to group. A group can be

127
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:45,960
isolated for maintenance from the rest of the plant. This makes for great

128
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:49,200
subtleness of operation.

129
00:14:55,459 --> 00:15:02,160
It takes 70 of these 20 stage groups to make up the 1,400 stages of the cascade.

130
00:15:02,260 --> 00:15:07,180
Groups are located on both sides of a central aisle in each of the plant

131
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:23,180
buildings. Buildings are designed on the scale of the huge units they house.

132
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:29,320
This diffuser when set up with its companions in a group of 20 will be streamed through

133
00:15:29,420 --> 00:15:36,380
by uranium hexafluoride at around 100 degrees centigrade. Almost throughout, gas is at lower

134
00:15:36,479 --> 00:15:40,399
than atmospheric pressure.

135
00:15:40,499 --> 00:15:48,820
But not all stages are the same size.

136
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:54,639
The complex theory of enrichment requires that throughout the cascade, from the natural

137
00:15:54,739 --> 00:16:05,039
uranium input to both ends, hexafluoride flow, instead of being even in all stages, must

138
00:16:05,140 --> 00:16:10,200
be greater near input and diminish progressively towards the extremities

139
00:16:10,300 --> 00:16:16,120
according to this ideal curve characteristic of enrichment plants.

140
00:16:17,300 --> 00:16:27,900
In theory each of the 1400 stages should be different. In practice to facilitate

141
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:33,980
stage mass production only three stage sizes have been selected.

142
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:43,980
Stages differ in height, weight, and compressor power.

143
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:48,980
Each size is set up in groups of 20.

144
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:57,980
Given these three stage sizes, the number of groups of each size was selected for optimum approximation to the theoretical curve.

145
00:16:58,080 --> 00:17:07,920
This accounts for the layout of the plant's 70 groups, 40 on the enrichment side and 30

146
00:17:08,020 --> 00:17:13,339
on the depletion side.

147
00:17:13,439 --> 00:17:18,680
All these groups are set up in the plant's four buildings.

148
00:17:18,780 --> 00:17:25,099
The largest houses the large size depletion groups.

149
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:31,320
The next houses the large size enrichment groups.

150
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:39,360
Then the same building houses the medium size enrichment

151
00:17:39,460 --> 00:17:44,200
and depletion groups.

152
00:17:44,300 --> 00:17:53,900
And the smallest building houses the small groups at both ends of the cascade.

153
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,480
So throughout the plant, all these adjacent stages with natural uranium as the starting

154
00:17:58,580 --> 00:18:04,460
point lead on the one side to uranium enriched to more than 3% and on the other to uranium

155
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:09,180
depleted to around 0.2%.

156
00:18:09,280 --> 00:18:23,140
The total making up the 70 groups and the 1400 stages of the cascade.

157
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:27,499
While in these four buildings the uranium hexafluoride is being diffused and re-diffused

158
00:18:27,599 --> 00:18:32,640
ceaselessly through thousands of kilometres of barriers, the heat transmitted to the gas

159
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:35,960
by compression must be removed.

160
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:38,620
This is the role of the cooling towers.

161
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:44,020
The water heated in the heat exchangers loses its calories here before being recycled to

162
00:18:44,120 --> 00:18:50,660
the plant in closed circuit.

163
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:56,580
enrichment requires electricity. The power and number of the 1400 compressors

164
00:18:56,680 --> 00:19:01,420
permanently absorbs the energy provided by three of the four reactors of the

165
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:07,480
nearby nuclear power station. Three reactors to run a plant which provides

166
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:12,019
enriched uranium for more than a hundred others. A plant which can assure

167
00:19:12,119 --> 00:19:17,480
long-term nuclear fuel supply for Belgium, Italy and Spain, France's

168
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:20,839
associates in this enterprise and for many other customers.

169
00:19:26,419 --> 00:19:29,660
Every year, these containers bring more than 25,000 tons

170
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:32,019
of uranium hexafluoride to the plant.

171
00:19:37,599 --> 00:19:42,660
At the plant exit, in smaller containers, the same chemical product comes out,

172
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:45,940
but with a far greater wealth of energy.

173
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:49,999
Soon, this low-enriched uranium will be fuel for a reactor

174
00:19:50,099 --> 00:19:54,700
after final, more traditional modifications and various industrial processes.

175
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:04,900
Now, it is in the form of tiny uranium oxide pellets,

176
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,600
soon to be placed into fuel rod assemblies.

177
00:20:16,040 --> 00:20:42,140
Every year, thanks to the Trikasta enrichment plant, about 5,000 such assemblies can be

178
